                "paragraphs": [
                    "Determinants aren't just abstract numbers; they have powerful geometric applications! We can build a matrix grid out of X,Y coordinates to calculate areas on a graph quickly.",
                    "**1. Area of a Triangle**",
                    "If you have the vertices of a triangle `(x1, y1)`, `(x2, y2)`, and `(x3, y3)`, you can stack them into a 3x3 determinant.",
                    "Add a column of `1`s on the far right to make it a square matrix: \n`C1: x's | C2: y's | C3: 1's`.",
                    "Calculate the determinant, and multiply it by `1/2`. \n**Absolute values matter:** Area is always positive! So if the determinant spits out `-10`, the area is `|-10| * 1/2 = 5` square units.",
                    "**2. Collinearity (The Straight Line Rule)**",
                    "If three points lie on the exact same straight line, they can't form a 2D triangle. Therefore, the \"triangle Area\" must be exactly 0.",
                    "If you load three coordinates into the area determinant formula and the result is `0`, you have PROVEN that the points are Collinear.",
                    "**3. Finding the Equation of a Line**",
                    "If given two points `(x1, y1)` and `(x2, y2)`, we can find the equation of the line passing through them. Let the third point just be generic variables `(x, y)`. Force the Area determinant to equal `0`, expand it, and you'll magically generate the algebraic equation of the line!"
                ],
                "formulas": [
                    {
                        "rule": "Area of Triangle using Determinants",
                        "formula": "\\( \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\begin{matrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{matrix} \\right| \\)",
                        "example": "Always remember to wrap the final answer in an absolute value (Area can't be negative)."
